ors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =0.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444334 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444368 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444334 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444369 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444334 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444370 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444334 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444371 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444334 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444372 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444334 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444334 1045 "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444334 1046 #edb/ref 17592186444371 13194139534