true] [17592186444363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444335 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444338 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444340 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444345 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444350 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444363 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444363 1077 "selection" 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444363 1078 #edb/ref 17592186444335 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444363 1079 #uuid "d5476fb6-80da-5fb0-9202-81c168d50130" 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444362 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444364 1069 "q-108000" 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444364 1071 "Given that $\\lambda =4$ and $\\lambda =5$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444364 1072 #uuid "4056bc20-f830-546e-b11d-9973d25fccb0" 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444364 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444365 1068 #edb