d{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444360 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444367 1069 "q-107270" 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444367 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4.$" 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444367 1072 #uuid "d0ad3502-048f-57c9-928b-2eb350ba84cc" 13194139534924 17592186045417 true] [17592186444367 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =4.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{a